WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
There is nothing more frustrating than tapping an app icon and waiting on a stalled screen.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.000
That sluggish inertia defines an unoptimized cold start.

3
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.200
For Android developers, the bedrock of a cold start is a well-understood sequence.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:21.800
The Android runtime compiles the code, the Dex files load, application.onCreateExecutes,

5
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.000
the main activity inflates the layout, and the first frame appears.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.000
This diagram compares the standard Android native start on top against a react native start on the bottom.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.000
The native sequence is identical, but interrupted by a massive inserted block,

8
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
JavaScript layer initialization, which delays the final layout.

9
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.000
React Native relies on the Hermes engine, pre-compiling JavaScript into bytecode ahead of time.

10
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.000
While execution is fast, reading that compiled bytecode from storage

11
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
and loading it into memory extracts a heavy time penalty.

12
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Pushing massive monolithic JavaScript bundles through that memory bottleneck

13
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
forces the engine into eager evaluation, spiking the time to interactive.

14
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
To compress the startup timeline, we must systematically dismantle that monolithic JavaScript initialization.

15
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
We achieve this by mapping the startup sequence using Flipper to isolate the exact execution bottlenecks.

16
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Once isolated, we apply specific architectural levers,

17
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:26.000
pruning bundle IO, implementing Metro inline requires, and leveraging RAM bundles.

18
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Optimizing react native startup means deferring code execution from application launch directly to the application runtime.

19
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:39.000
Moving beyond rawperformance.now logs requires analyzing visual execution waterfalls.

20
00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.000
This is a Flipper execution waterfall chart generated by the react native performance plugin.

21
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
It defines the cold start through four sequential cascading blocks.

22
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.000
The first block native module setup is the native initialization phase.

23
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
This is strictly constrained by standard Android optimizations,

24
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.000
like deferring non-critical work in mainapplication.kt.

25
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.000
The second block is bundle load.

26
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.000
This is the physical phase where the Hermes engine reads the pre-compiled bytecode from the file system

27
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.000
and loads it into memory.

28
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:15.000
The third block, JS execution, represents the heavy lifting of executing the global JavaScript scope.

29
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.000
The final milestone is content appeared.

30
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000
The exact moment the first meaningful frame is successfully rendered to the user.

31
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.000
Bundle load and JS execution are the two primary JavaScript centric bottlenecks.

32
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Engineers have total control over both.

33
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
Mathematically shrinking these two specific profiling blocks is the only guaranteed pathway to achieving a sub-second time to interactive.

34
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.000
We start by targeting the first major isolated bottleneck, the bundle load phase.

35
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:47.000
The duration of this phase is dictated by a strict physical constraint.

36
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.000
Low time is proportional to the physical size of the JavaScript payload stored on disk.

37
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.000
To audit this, we need baseline metrics for a healthy, uncompressed, react native bundle.

38
00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.000
Under one megabyte is good.

39
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:01.000
One to three megabytes is acceptable.

40
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:04.000
Exceeding three megabytes requires immediate triage.

41
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.000
This is a tree map visualization generated by the react native bundle visualizer.

42
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.000
It maps out a complete JavaScript bundle footprint.

43
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.000
One dominant block is clearly visible, moment.js, weighing 240 kilobytes.

44
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Monolithic legacy libraries command a massive memory footprint, and the Hermes engine is forced to load them in their entirety before the app can start.

45
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Replacing that dependency with a modern alternative like date FNS instantly shrinks that 240 kilobytes block down to a tiny fractional sliver of 10 to 20 kilobytes.

46
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:47.000
Another common payload trap is importing an entire utility library, like load ash, rather than explicitly targeting the specific functions you actually need.

47
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:55.000
Eliminating duplicate dependencies and stripping out unused families in large icon sets directly reduces the total memory IO overhead.

48
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:04.000
A smaller physical payload allows Hermes to load the bytecode into memory significantly faster, directly accelerating the bundle load phase.

49
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.000
With the payload pruned, we transition to the next critical block, the JS execution phase.

50
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.000
By default, the JavaScript engine eager evaluates every top-level import during initialization.

51
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Loading business logic for screens a user hasn't even navigated to, unnecessarily balloons the time spent in this execution phase.

52
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.000
This code snippet shows a standard top-level import.

53
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Metro Bundler provides an automated solution for deferring this loading through a feature called inline requires.

54
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.000
During the build process, Metro intercepts the standard top-level imports.

55
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:47.000
It seamlessly re-writes them, moving the call down into a localized require nestled deep inside a specific function, evaluating it only at the exact moment of invocation.

56
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Developers continue writing standard imports at the top of their files.

57
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.000
Metro handles the complex transformations silently in the background.

58
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:03.000
Forcing this deferral prevents unused modules from blocking the JavaScript thread at launch, massively shrinking the JS execution block.

59
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Inline requires fixed module-level eager evaluation, but extremely large apps require broader architectural deferral.

60
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:20.000
You can use react.lazy for dynamic screen loading, explicitly targeting rarely visited UI components like admin panels or deeply nested settings menus.

61
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:28.000
But at the extreme scale, enterprise applications can easily exceed the five megabyte bundle threshold, even after aggressive pruning.

62
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.000
This architectural flow diagram illustrates RAM bundles, which change how the system loads the payload.

63
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.000
The monolithic six megabyte bundle is no longer a single unit.

64
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.000
It is physically split into dozens of smaller distinct modules.

65
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:52.000
RAM bundles pre-split these modules at build time, enabling this requires explicit configurations in both build.gradle and metro.config.js.

66
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:58.000
This split allows the file system to load only the exact entry point and immediate dependencies needed at launch.

67
00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.000
The vast majority of the code base is left behind until it is explicitly requested.

68
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:12.000
RAM bundles ensure that even as an application's total code base scales infinitely, the actual cold start memory payload remains artificially small.

69
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.000
Returning to the flipper waterfall chart, we map the compounding impact of these optimization techniques.

70
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Pruning dependencies compress the bundle load phase, automating inline requires slash to the JS execution phase.

71
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:30.000
Simultaneously, native optimizations chipped away at the native module setup block.

72
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:41.000
To squeeze out the final milliseconds, ensure local assets use 2x and 3x variants so metro can automatically serve the exact necessary resolution.

73
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:49.000
A slow react native startup is not an inherent platform flaw. It is simply poor management of execution priority.

74
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:58.000
By strictly auditing these profiling blocks, engineers can confidently achieve a near-native time to interactive on cross-platform architectures.

75
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:06.000
Once you have achieved peak performance, the engineering requirement shifts to guaranteeing long-term architectural stability.

76
00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:17.000
Next, we map the testing stack, translating familiar Android paradigms like JUnit and Espresso into react natives just testing library and detox.

